# Countries found from a city on the world map, I want to go to, and a reason.
lesson language-where-to-go v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=9 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Where do you want to go?"
  goal "Name countries in [[language.name]], say where you want to go and why, and what you can see or eat there."

  look {
    scene 36x19 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        worldmap map names=0 blank=-1 oceans=0 places=["Tokyo", "Rome", "Cairo", "Mexico City"] numbered=0 route=0
      }
    }
    say """
      To say where you want to go, say [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-italy]], I want to go to Italy. Then
      give a reason: [[travel.i-want-to-see-the-colosseum]], I want to see the Colosseum, or
      [[travel.i-want-to-eat-pizza]], I want to eat pizza. To ask, say
      [[phrase.where-do-you-want-to-go]], where do you want to go?
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.where-to-go-name k=4
    }
    show language.where-to-go-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.where-to-go-meaning k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.where-to-go-write k=2 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.where-to-go-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.where-to-go-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.where-to-go-write k=3 easy-k=4 hard-k=5
    show language.where-to-go-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.where-to-go-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.where-to-go-write k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.where-to-go-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.where-to-go-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.where-do-you-want-to-go]] Where do you want to go? [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-italy]] I want to go to Italy."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two neighbouring countries taken for each other, China and Japan,
    Italy and Greece: each naming question marks a capital city on the world map and offers the
    neighbour beside the right country. The cities are the world map drawing's own, and the pyramids
    in Egypt, Mount Fuji in Japan and the Colosseum in Rome are recalled. The step up from because
    in grade four is a wish about another country with a reason, and what one can do there. From
    this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is
    not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, find each city on a globe and say its country aloud. Off the
    page, plan a dream journey on a map, one sentence for each country. It meets England's key stage
    2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and
    answer questions; express opinions and respond to those of others"; Japan's grades 5 and 6
    foreign languages: "Can exchange their own thoughts and feelings regarding familiar and simple
    matters in daily life by using simple words and phrases and basic expressions" (Japan's grade
    five book We Can! 1 has a unit on where you want to go, recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can understand
    sentences and frequently used expressions related to areas of most immediate relevance (e.g.
    very basic personal and family information, shopping, local geography, employment)". The phrases
    are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-china]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-japan]] and [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-italy]] and the words to write are [[travel.greece]] and [[travel.egypt]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-russia]] and [[travel.i-want-to-go-to-greece]] and the words to write are [[travel.egypt]], [[travel.mexico]] and [[travel.japan]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
